471828-+
471829-+## Reviewer findings resolved
471830-+
471831-+### In-flight journal generations
471832-+
471833-+Transaction journals now expose marker generations in a separate group for
471834-+each context target. A group covers its live, staged, staged-source, backup,
471835-+rollback, cleanup, and cleanup-tomb paths. GC snapshots those paths before and
471836-+after marker reads, requires at least one valid marker in every group, and
471837-+merges references only from a fully safe group. A missing group, corrupt or
471838-+unreadable marker, path race, journal-root race, or journal filesystem error
--
471843-+vanishing, a generation disappearing during the scan, a forced journal-root
471844-+`OSError`, and one valid project target alongside vanished global and hybrid
471845-+targets so unrelated context state cannot mask uncertainty.
471846-+
471847-+### Exact protected-object retirement
471848-+
471849-+The POSIX collector carries the classified directory state into quarantine,
471850-+selects it through the held driver descriptor, and verifies the quarantined
471851-+inode before deletion. A pathname exchange is restored or retained; a driver
471852-+root exchange can sweep only the entry reached through the previously held
471853-+root descriptor and never the new live root.
471854-+
471855-+The Windows collector reopens and records the verified object identity, opens
471856-+the selected source with `DELETE`, and renames that exact open handle with
471857:+`SetFileInformationByHandle(FileRenameInfo)`. The destination is relative to
471858-+the already-held quarantine directory, and replacement is disabled. Entry and
471859-+root exchange tests plant a valid young replacement between classification and
471860-+retirement and assert that it remains a cache hit.
471861-+
471862-+### Runtime orphan compatibility
471863-+
471864-+Orphan matching is limited to manager-owned legacy and indexed temporary and
471865-+backup names plus indexed stale names. PID and allocation indexes must use the
471866-+canonical numeric shape. Live-PID entries and malformed, leading-zero, or
471867-+unrelated dotfiles are retained; only exact dead-PID names are swept.
471868-+
471869-+### Native Windows coverage
471870-+
471871-+The fake build fixture now derives the native target and artifact suffix on
--
471878-+
471879-+The previously accepted currentness behavior was preserved: marker-v1 schemas
471880-+1–5, raw-snapshot and static-context replanning, complete target/toolchain/
471881-+execution-policy cache identity, protected receipt/artifact and shim checks,
471882-+result-only capability evidence, and normal-install rebuild/repair remain
471883-+unchanged by this rework.
471884-+
471885-+## Fact-checking and sources
471886-+
471887-+- The task behavior remains checked against the Curator manager requirements
471888-+  for [status and locked fail-safe GC](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L657-L684),
471889-+  [cache acceptance and rebuild](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L329-L340),
471890-+  and [normal-install repair](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L444-L463).
471891-+- Microsoft documents that the file handle passed to
471892:+  [`SetFileInformationByHandle`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-setfileinformationbyhandle)
471893-+  must carry the access needed for the requested change and that
471894-+  `FileRenameInfo` is the rename information class.
471895-+- Microsoft's
471896-+  [`FILE_RENAME_INFORMATION`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/ntifs/ns-ntifs-_file_rename_information)
471897-+  contract states that rename acts on the supplied file handle, requires
471898-+  `DELETE` access, can resolve a relative name through a held root-directory
471899-+  handle, and fails on an existing target when replacement is false. It also
471900-+  defines the variable-length buffer sizing used by the implementation.
471901-+
471902-+## Validation evidence
471903-+
471904-+Every gate below was launched as a standalone process without `tee` or an
471905-+exit-status-obscuring pipe.
