493877-+
493878-+## Reviewer findings resolved
493879-+
493880-+### In-flight journal generations
493881-+
493882-+Transaction journals now expose marker generations in a separate group for
493883-+each context target. A group covers its live, staged, staged-source, backup,
493884-+rollback, cleanup, and cleanup-tomb paths. GC snapshots those paths before and
493885-+after marker reads, requires at least one valid marker in every group, and
493886-+merges references only from a fully safe group. A missing group, corrupt or
493887-+unreadable marker, path race, journal-root race, or journal filesystem error
--
493892-+vanishing, a generation disappearing during the scan, a forced journal-root
493893-+`OSError`, and one valid project target alongside vanished global and hybrid
493894-+targets so unrelated context state cannot mask uncertainty.
493895-+
493896-+### Exact protected-object retirement
493897-+
493898-+The POSIX collector carries the classified directory state into quarantine,
493899-+selects it through the held driver descriptor, and verifies the quarantined
493900-+inode before deletion. A pathname exchange is restored or retained; a driver
493901-+root exchange can sweep only the entry reached through the previously held
493902-+root descriptor and never the new live root.
493903-+
493904-+The Windows collector reopens and records the verified object identity, opens
493905-+the selected source with `DELETE`, and renames that exact open handle with
493906:+`SetFileInformationByHandle(FileRenameInfo)`. The destination is relative to
493907-+the already-held quarantine directory, and replacement is disabled. Entry and
493908-+root exchange tests plant a valid young replacement between classification and
493909-+retirement and assert that it remains a cache hit.
493910-+
493911-+### Runtime orphan compatibility
493912-+
493913-+Orphan matching is limited to manager-owned legacy and indexed temporary and
493914-+backup names plus indexed stale names. PID and allocation indexes must use the
493915-+canonical numeric shape. Live-PID entries and malformed, leading-zero, or
493916-+unrelated dotfiles are retained; only exact dead-PID names are swept.
493917-+
493918-+### Native Windows coverage
493919-+
493920-+The fake build fixture now derives the native target and artifact suffix on
--
493927-+
493928-+The previously accepted currentness behavior was preserved: marker-v1 schemas
493929-+1–5, raw-snapshot and static-context replanning, complete target/toolchain/
493930-+execution-policy cache identity, protected receipt/artifact and shim checks,
493931-+result-only capability evidence, and normal-install rebuild/repair remain
493932-+unchanged by this rework.
493933-+
493934-+## Fact-checking and sources
493935-+
493936-+- The task behavior remains checked against the Curator manager requirements
493937-+  for [status and locked fail-safe GC](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L657-L684),
493938-+  [cache acceptance and rebuild](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L329-L340),
493939-+  and [normal-install repair](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/f5d7673039226ab81de2f4f87e2155ae995c4df3/profiles/manager.md#L444-L463).
493940-+- Microsoft documents that the file handle passed to
493941:+  [`SetFileInformationByHandle`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-setfileinformationbyhandle)
493942-+  must carry the access needed for the requested change and that
493943-+  `FileRenameInfo` is the rename information class.
493944-+- Microsoft's
493945-+  [`FILE_RENAME_INFORMATION`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/ddi/ntifs/ns-ntifs-_file_rename_information)
493946-+  contract states that rename acts on the supplied file handle, requires
493947-+  `DELETE` access, can resolve a relative name through a held root-directory
493948-+  handle, and fails on an existing target when replacement is false. It also
493949-+  defines the variable-length buffer sizing used by the implementation.
493950-+
