{"body":"`RootDirectory` is not universally required to be NULL: it can be used as a directory handle to resolve a relative `FileName` for the rename operation. A more accurate description would explain when NULL is appropriate (common case) versus when providing a directory handle is meaningful (relative target name resolution). As written, this could mislead readers into thinking non-NULL is invalid.\n```suggestion\nSet to \u003cb\u003eNULL\u003c/b\u003e in the common case. If \u003cb\u003eFileName\u003c/b\u003e specifies a relative name, this field can be a handle to the directory relative to which the new name is resolved.\n```","created_at":"2026-04-15T18:38:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/sdk-api/pull/1123#discussion_r3088627003","line":null,"path":"sdk-api-src/content/winbase/ns-winbase-file_rename_info.md","user":"Copilot"}
{"body":"For `FILE_RENAME_INFO`, `FileName` is a trailing inline buffer and is commonly treated as not requiring a terminating null; `FileNameLength` is typically documented/used as the length in bytes of the file name buffer content (often excluding any terminator). Stating it includes the terminating null byte is likely incorrect and can lead to off-by-two (UTF-16) or buffer sizing errors when callers allocate/initialize the struct. Suggest aligning this with Windows header/doc behavior by describing the length in bytes of `FileName` and clarifying whether a terminator is required (usually not).\n```suggestion\nThe size of \u003cb\u003eFileName\u003c/b\u003e, in bytes. A terminating null character is not required.\n```","created_at":"2026-04-15T18:38:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/sdk-api/pull/1123#discussion_r3088627032","line":null,"path":"sdk-api-src/content/winbase/ns-winbase-file_rename_info.md","user":"Copilot"}
